<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles the Bald</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">823–877</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Charles II of France · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-dynasty">Carolingian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles the Bald was the youngest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a>. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-verdun">Treaty of Verdun</a> in 843 he received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-francia">West Francia</a>, the forerunner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and in 875 he was crowned emperor. His reign was troubled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raids and by Breton and Aquitanian rebels, and to defend the realm he relied on great counts, such as those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, who later became hereditary princes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald.jpg" alt="Charles the Bald" width="320" height="381" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Comte Vivien - Moines de l'abbaye Saint-Martin de Tours, vers 850 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Biblioth%C3%A8que_nationale_de_France_-_Bible_de_Vivien_Ms._Latin_1_folio_423r_d%C3%A9tail_Le_comte_Vivien_offre_le_manuscrit_de_la_Bible_faite_%C3%A0_l%27abbaye_de_Saint-Martin_de_Tours_%C3%A0_Charles_le_Chauve.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Charles was born on 13 June 823 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>, the youngest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a> by his second wife, Judith. His elder brothers were already adults with sub-kingdoms of their own, and his father's attempts to give him one, first Alemannia and then the country between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>, failed in the face of rebellions by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-i-of-aquitaine">Pepin I of Aquitaine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a>. At a diet in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> in 837 Louis the Pious had the nobles do homage to Charles as heir to all that was once <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. When Louis died in 840 war broke out between his sons.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Charles allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a> against the new Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair I</a>, and they defeated Lothair at Fontenoy-en-Puisaye on 25 June 841. In 842 the brothers confirmed their alliance by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oaths-of-strasbourg">Oaths of Strasbourg</a>, and the war ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-verdun">Treaty of Verdun</a> in August 843, which gave Charles the western kingdom, roughly modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> as far as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saone">Saône</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a>, with the Spanish March as far as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a>. His reign was troubled. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bretons">Bretons</a> under Nomenoë and Erispoë defeated him at Ballon in 845 and Jengland in 851 and gained de facto independence.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> devastated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> valleys, and after they sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 845 Charles had to buy their retreat at a heavy price. By the Edict of Pistres of 864 he gave the army a cavalry element and ordered fortified bridges built across rivers; two of them saved Paris during the siege of 885 to 886. After the death of his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-ii">Lothair II</a> in 869 he had himself consecrated king of Lotharingia at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>, but under pressure from Louis he agreed in 870 to partition it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the death of Emperor Louis II, Charles travelled south with the support of Pope John VIII, was crowned king at Pavia, and received the imperial insignia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on 25 December 875. He was beaten by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a>'s forces at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andernach">Andernach</a> on 8 October 876, and a second expedition at the pope's call found little support among the nobles. Ill, he turned back and died at Brides-les-Bains on 6 October 877.</p><p>His son Louis succeeded him. His nickname may have been ironic, for contemporary images show him with a full head of hair; one suggestion links it to his early lack of a kingdom. His seal as emperor bore the words renovatio imperii <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Romani</a> et Francorum, renewal of the empire of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charles the Bald” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He was constantly renegotiating the division of the empire among Lothair, Pepin, Louis the German and, after 823, his youngest son <b>Charles the Bald</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 862 Baldwin, a Frankish noble later called Iron Arm, eloped with Judith, daughter of King <b>Charles the Bald</b> and already the widow of two kings of Wessex.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-flanders">County of Flanders · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…the Ordinatio imperii of 817 to keep the empire united under his eldest son, Lothar I, but the birth of another son, <b>Charles the Bald</b>, in 823 and attempts to provide for him provoked rebellions by his elder sons in 830 and 833, when Louis was briefly deposed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Near Grand-Fougeray on the border of Brittany, the Breton army of Erispoe defeated the West Frankish king <b>Charles the Bald</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-22">22 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Charles the Bald</b> and Louis the German divided the lands of their late nephew Lothair II between West and East Francia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-8">8 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Louis the German and <b>Charles the Bald</b> pledged alliance against their brother Lothair, each swearing in the other's language.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-14">14 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Avrieux, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.11,8.68&amp;year=858" aria-label="Frankfurt on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Charles the Bald"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5474 5810h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5474 5810h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.11,8.68&amp;year=858">What was here in 858 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
